      "19233": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "text": "unzip(unzip = \"internal\") leaks the open zip handle, and the file descriptor it owns, whenever extraction fails with an R error.\n\nzipunzip() in src/main/dounzip.c opens the archive with unzOpen64() and closes it only on its normal return. extract_one() calls error() when the output file cannot be opened (\"cannot open file '...': File name too long\", a read-only exdir, ...), and that error unwinds past the unzClose(), so the unz64_s handle and its FILE are lost. Each failed call costs one descriptor (checked by counting /dev/fd across repeated calls on R 4.6.1 and R-devel r90728), and under AddressSanitizer it shows up as a 248-byte direct leak from unzOpenInternal(), which is how OSS-Fuzz found it. The same path is taken by any other error raised inside the loop, e.g. the \"skipped ../ path component(s)\" warning under options(warn = 2). unz_open() for unz() connections has a smaller instance of the same shape: its \"cannot locate file\" warning is issued before the handle is closed.\n\nProposed patch: attached as unzip-handle-leak.diff (against r90728). It registers the close in a CTXT_CCODE context in zipunzip() so an error unwind runs unzClose(), as connections.c does for the connections it opens, and has unz_open() close the handle before warning. On a patched build the descriptor count stays flat across failed calls, and extraction, listing and unz() reads are unchanged.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/387, where CI results can be seen.",
